  [1mThe fork:[0m at the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called, nothing in this app knows a user ID. The page has a username field and a
  password field; a real user ID is something a backend assigns [3mafter[0m it authenticates you. So "add a userId parameter"
  can mean three different things, and they don't cost the same later.

  - [1mReturn the ID instead of passing it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m keeps its two-argument signature and its return value grows a [38;5;153muserId[39m the
    (future) server hands back; the submit handler logs that. This is what "track who logged in" usually turns out to
    mean, it matches how the real endpoint at [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m would behave, and it changes no call sites. Costs you nothing
    if you later decide you [3malso[0m want a caller-supplied ID.
  - [1mAdd an optional third parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m, defaulting to [38;5;153mnull[39m. The signature widens now,
    the one existing caller passes nothing, and the plumbing is ready for whatever source you have in mind. Cheap to
    add, but an optional parameter nobody fills in tends to stay empty, and an unused parameter is harder to delete
    later than to not add.
  - [1mAdd a required third parameter plus a source for it[0m — the form gains a field (or the app reads an ID from
    config/session) and the handler passes it. This is the only option that actually makes a caller-supplied ID real,
    but it's the biggest change: it touches [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, the handler, and [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m, and it breaks the current call
    signature.

  My recommendation is the first. "So we can track who logged in" is a tracking goal, and the identity you want to track
  is the one the login attempt [3mresolves to[0m — which only the response can tell you. The second option looks like the
  literal reading of your request but ships a parameter with no value to put in it.

  One small thing regardless of which you pick: [38;5;153mlogin[39m already [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms the username, and whatever we add will get
  logged alongside it. That's fine for a stub, worth remembering before this talks to a real endpoint.
